Goodevening Manager (gm) - installation on another Windows 11 computer
=====================================================================

1. Files to copy from ./target
------------------------------

Only ONE file is required:

    target\release\gm.exe

Everything else the program needs is built into gm.exe:
  * interface languages (assets\lang\*.lng) - embedded at compile time;
  * help files (assets\help\*.hlf)          - embedded at compile time;
  * all modules (archive, temp panel, process panel, network panel,
    compare, command prefixes, editor/panel tools, shell menu) - compiled in;
  * SQLite (used for state.db) - statically linked ("bundled").

Do NOT copy anything else from target\release:
  * gm.pdb      - debug symbols, only useful for debugging crashes (optional);
  * gm.d        - Cargo dependency list, build-only;
  * build\, deps\, examples\, incremental\, .fingerprint\, .cargo-lock
                - intermediate build files, not needed at runtime.

The assets\ folder of the source tree is NOT needed at runtime either.

Place gm.exe in any folder, for example:

    C:\Program Files\gm\gm.exe        (system-wide, needs administrator rights)
    %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\gm\gm.exe (current user only)
    D:\Tools\gm\gm.exe                (portable use, see section 4)


2. Required action before the first run: Visual C++ Runtime
-----------------------------------------------------------

gm.exe is built with the MSVC toolchain (x86_64-pc-windows-msvc) and imports
VCRUNTIME140.dll. This DLL is NOT part of a clean Windows 11 installation.

Install the "Microsoft Visual C++ Redistributable for Visual Studio
2015-2022" (x64) on the target computer:

    https://aka.ms/vs/17/release/vc_redist.x64.exe

or, from an administrator command prompt / PowerShell:

    winget install Microsoft.VCRedist.2015+.x64

If it is missing, Windows shows the error
"The code execution cannot proceed because VCRUNTIME140.dll was not found."

Many computers already have it (it comes with many applications and games);
installing it again is harmless.

All other DLLs used by gm.exe are standard parts of Windows 11 and need no
action: kernel32, user32, shell32, advapi32, ole32, oleaut32, combase,
ntdll, mpr, pdh, powrprof, psapi, bcryptprimitives and the Universal C
Runtime (api-ms-win-crt-*.dll).

Alternative (no redistributable needed): build gm.exe with the C runtime
linked statically, then only gm.exe has to be copied:

    set RUSTFLAGS=-C target-feature=+crt-static
    cargo build --release

(in PowerShell: $env:RUSTFLAGS = "-C target-feature=+crt-static")


3. Optional: run gm from any folder
-----------------------------------

Add the folder that contains gm.exe to the PATH environment variable:
Settings -> System -> About -> Advanced system settings ->
Environment Variables -> Path -> Edit -> New -> (folder of gm.exe).
Open a new terminal window afterwards and type:

    gm

gm is a console program. Start it from Windows Terminal (the default
terminal of Windows 11, recommended - true colour is detected
automatically), from cmd.exe or from PowerShell. Double-clicking gm.exe
in Explorer also works and opens it in a console window.


4. Settings and data (created automatically)
--------------------------------------------

Nothing has to be created by hand. On the first start gm creates its
profile folder:

    %APPDATA%\gm\        (e.g. C:\Users\<name>\AppData\Roaming\gm)

with settings.toml, state.db, and optionally keymap.toml and GmMenu.ini.

To move your existing settings to the new computer, copy that folder from
the old computer to the same location on the new one.

Portable mode (e.g. on a USB stick): start gm with

    gm -p          (or gm --portable)

and the settings are kept in a gm-profile folder next to gm.exe. In this
case gm.exe must be in a folder where the user has write permission
(not C:\Program Files). Another folder can be chosen with
gm --profile <dir>.


5. Summary
----------

  1. Copy target\release\gm.exe to the target computer.
  2. Install the Visual C++ Redistributable x64 (unless gm.exe was built
     with +crt-static).
  3. (Optional) Add the folder of gm.exe to PATH.
  4. Run gm in Windows Terminal.
